+### Medical Imaging Infrastructure for Dental Workflows
+Dentists working with CBCT, DICOM, or multidisciplinary imaging often need general medical-imaging infrastructure. These repos are not dentist-only, but they are among the most practical open-source foundations for real dental deployments.
+
+#### [Slicer](https://github.com/Slicer/Slicer)
+> **Description:** 3D Slicer is a full medical image computing platform rather than a narrow AI model, and that is exactly why so many dental projects build on it. The README emphasizes visualization, image computing, extensibility, and cross-platform deployment, which makes it useful for dental CBCT review, segmentation pipelines, and extension-based workflows. It is mature, heavily used in research, and flexible enough for orthodontics, oral surgery, and implant planning. GitHub lists C++ as primary, a BSD-style license, about 2,191 stars, and activity updated on 2025-11-29.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/Slicer/Slicer](https://github.com/Slicer/Slicer)
+- **Stars:** 2,191 ⭐
+- **Language:** C++
+- **License:** BSD-3-Clause
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-11-29
+- **Category:** dicom, visualization
+- **Best for:** Clinics and labs that need a durable open-source platform for CBCT and 3D imaging workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [OHIF Viewers](https://github.com/OHIF/Viewers)
+> **Description:** OHIF Viewers provides a web-based imaging viewer stack for DICOM studies and is one of the strongest open-source choices for browser-based review. The README focuses on extensibility, standards support, and radiology-style workflows, but the same architecture can support dental imaging portals, second-opinion review, and multi-site image access. It is especially useful when a team wants zero local installs for image review across chairs, offices, or specialists. GitHub shows JavaScript/TypeScript as primary, MIT licensing, about 4,000 stars, and recent activity through late 2025.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/OHIF/Viewers](https://github.com/OHIF/Viewers)
+- **Stars:** 4,000 ⭐
