+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for dentists in 2026?
+The strongest starting stack is usually `3D Slicer` or `OHIF` for imaging, `pydicom` and `pynetdicom` for DICOM workflows, `Whisper` or `whisper.cpp` for dictation, and `PaddleOCR` or `Docling` for document intake. Dental-native repos exist, but the field is still small compared with general healthcare AI.
+
+### Are there truly dentist-specific AI GitHub repositories?
+Yes, but not many mature ones. Repos like `SlicerAutomatedDentalTools`, `SlicerDentalModelSeg`, `ToothGroupNetwork`, `SlicerCBCTToothSegmentation`, and `deep-dental-image` are directly dental-relevant, but the ecosystem is still a gap compared with radiology or general medical imaging.
+
+### Can a dental clinic self-host these AI tools without sending PHI to the cloud?
+Many of them, yes. `whisper.cpp`, `Vosk`, `Ollama`, `Open WebUI`, `AnythingLLM`, `pydicom`, and `OHIF` can all be part of a self-hosted deployment, though the final compliance posture depends on how you store data, logs, backups, and user access.
+
+### What open source AI repo is best for dental X-ray or CBCT analysis?
+For the platform layer, start with `3D Slicer`, `OHIF`, and `MONAI Label`. For dental-specific segmentation and craniofacial work, evaluate `SlicerAutomatedDentalTools`, `SlicerDentalModelSeg`, `SlicerCBCTToothSegmentation`, and `ToothGroupNetwork`.
+
+### What is the best open source speech-to-text tool for dentists?
+`Whisper` is the strongest accuracy baseline, `whisper.cpp` is often the best offline deployment option, and `WhisperLive` is a good choice for near-real-time transcription. `Vosk` remains useful where low-latency offline speech matters more than absolute transcription quality.
+
+### Which GitHub repos help dentists automate paperwork and intake forms?
+`PaddleOCR`, `docTR`, `Docling`, `unstructured`, and `Tesseract` are the main options in this list. Use OCR-first tools for extraction and parsing-first tools for searchable internal knowledge bases and RAG.
+
