+
+# Best AI Tools for Dentists in 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-dentists-2026?style=social)
+
+> Best AI tools for dentists in 2026 still skew heavily toward imaging, transcription, and self-hosted workflow infrastructure rather than dentist-only SaaS products. This list focuses only on public GitHub repositories, with dental-specific projects first and adjacent open source stacks second, so dental clinics, labs, and researchers can evaluate practical tools without proprietary lock-in.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `3D Slicer`, `OHIF`, and `pydicom` are the strongest open-source foundations for dental CBCT, DICOM review, and imaging workflows.
+- `Whisper`, `whisper.cpp`, and `WhisperLive` are the best open-source options for chairside dictation and clinical note drafting.
+- `PaddleOCR`, `docTR`, and `Docling` are the most practical tools for forms, scanned referrals, insurance documents, and intake packets.
+- `Ollama`, `Open WebUI`, and `AnythingLLM` are strong self-hosted building blocks for local patient FAQ bots, SOP copilots, and RAG search.
+- Pure dental-native AI repos are still a gap, so this list combines verified dental projects with adjacent tools dentists can actually deploy today.
+
+## Why This List
+Most searches for the best AI tools for dentists surface proprietary products, but many practices need auditable, self-hosted, GitHub-based software for imaging, dictation, privacy, and workflow automation. Open source matters here because dental data is sensitive, workflows differ by clinic, and long-term cost control matters. The reality in 2026 is that dentist-native GitHub repos remain limited, so this list is honest about that gap and broadens into public repositories that fit real dental use cases.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Dental Imaging and Segmentation
+This category is the closest match to dentist-native AI on GitHub. It is still thinner than mainstream medical imaging, but these repos are directly relevant to dental scans, tooth segmentation, and craniofacial analysis.
+
+#### [SlicerAutomatedDentalTools](https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools)
+> **Description:** This 3D Slicer extension packages automated dental and craniofacial analysis into a GUI so users can run segmentation, landmark identification, and scan orientation without building custom pipelines from scratch. The README centers AMASSS, ALI-CBCT, and ALI-IOS workflows and positions the project as a practical front end for dental imaging teams. GitHub shows it as a Python project under Apache-2.0, with about 133 stars and recent activity updated on 2025-11-04, which makes it one of the more current dental-specific repos on this list.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools](https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools)
+- **Stars:** 133 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-11-04
+- **Category:** dental-imaging, cbct
+- **Best for:** Dental schools or specialists who want a Slicer-based workflow for CBCT and IOS analysis
+
+---
+
+#### [SlicerDentalModelSeg](https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerDentalModelSeg)
+> **Description:** SlicerDentalModelSeg adds a graphical workflow around deep-learning tooth segmentation inside 3D Slicer. The README frames it as a bridge between academic dental segmentation research and a usable extension for clinicians and researchers working with intraoral or model data. It is especially useful when a practice or university already standardizes on Slicer and wants a more focused dental module instead of assembling multiple scripts manually. GitHub lists it as Python, Apache-2.0, roughly 69 stars, and updated on 2025-04-30.
